    "result_text": "I added `is_valid_email` to `lnbits/helpers.py` and used it in the account update endpoint. I haven't run the tests or the app.\n\n- **`is_valid_email`:** it matches the whole string against an email regex, using `re.fullmatch`. I built it from the catalog pattern `validators.regex_match@v1`, whose marker comment is kept above it. The pattern's stock regex is a username regex that rejects `@`, so I swapped in an email regex. `check_code` flagged only that literal change, as low severity, so no approval is needed.\n- **`UpdateUser` model:** it had no `email` field, so I added `email: str | None = None` in `lnbits/core/models/users.py`.\n- **`PATCH /auth` handler:** it now returns a 400 \"Invalid email.\" if the email fails validation, and otherwise saves it to the account. Saving the email there is new behavior, and I didn't add a verification step.\n\nSeparately, `lnbits/helpers.py` has no `is_valid_email_address`, yet `auth_api.py`, `users.py`, `notifications.py` and the unit tests all import it. Those imports look broken as the repo stands. I left them alone, so you may want to make them use `is_valid_email` or restore the old function."
  },
